## Introduction

This chart deploys the GPUStack Operator control plane (`gpustack-operator-worker`), the
per-manufacturer device managers (`gpustack-operator-device-manager-<manufacturer>`), and the
applications the scheduling chain is built on — [Kueue](https://kueue.sigs.k8s.io),
[Node Feature Discovery](https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/node-feature-discovery) and the NFS/S3
CSI drivers — onto a Kubernetes cluster using the [Helm](https://helm.sh) package manager.

The operator turns raw node hardware into a Kueue-based scheduling chain for accelerators
(GPU/NPU/TPU).

> Those four applications are **vendored subcharts** of this chart, each behind an `enabled` switch,
> and they are deployed as part of **this** release. Two consequences worth knowing before you
> install:
>
> - `helm uninstall` deletes Kueue's CRDs, and therefore **every ClusterQueue, LocalQueue,
>   ResourceFlavor, AdmissionCheck and Workload in the cluster**. To keep a Kueue this release does
>   not own, install with `--set kueue.enabled=false` and bring your own. The same applies to
>   `node-feature-discovery.enabled=false`, which still gives you the `gpustack-cpu-info`
>   NodeFeatureRule the chain starts from.
> - Upgrading an install from v0.7.x or earlier, where these ran as Helm releases of their own, is a
>   **one-time ownership transfer**. See the
>   [migration guide](https://github.com/gpustack/gpustack-operator/blob/main/docs/operate/migration/to-subcharts.md).

Running more than one replica of each control-plane component is described in the
[high-availability guide](https://github.com/gpustack/gpustack-operator/blob/main/docs/operate/high-availability.md).

## Prerequisites

{{ template "chart.kubeVersionLine" . }}
- Kubernetes 1.29+ for the Kueue scheduling chain the bundled Kueue v0.18 requires; `1.23` is the install floor, and what works from `1.23` to `1.28` is not verified by any test
- Helm 3.8.0+ — or **3.21.0+** for the one-time upgrade of a v0.7.x-or-earlier install, which needs `--take-ownership`
- (Optional) [cert-manager](https://cert-manager.io) when `global.certmanager.enabled` is `"true"` (or `"auto"` with cert-manager present); it then issues every serving certificate this release needs, the bundled Kueue's included

## Installing the Chart

To install the chart with the release name `gpustack-operator`:

```bash
helm repo add gpustack https://docs.gpustack.ai/gpustack-operator/charts
helm install gpustack-operator gpustack/gpustack-operator \
  --namespace gpustack-system \
  --create-namespace
```

> Install with the release name `gpustack-operator` so that the rendered resource names
> match the conventional `gpustack-operator-worker` / `gpustack-operator-device-manager`.

## Upgrading the Chart

Upgrade with `--reset-then-reuse-values` (Helm 3.14+):

```bash
helm upgrade gpustack-operator gpustack/gpustack-operator \
  --namespace gpustack-system \
  --reset-then-reuse-values
```

It keeps the values you have set, at install and on every upgrade since, and takes every other value
from the new chart. `--reuse-values` does not: it carries the release's computed values forward, the
old chart's defaults included, so a default the new chart changes never reaches the release. A release
already upgraded that way picks the new defaults up on its next `--reset-then-reuse-values` upgrade.
A plain `--values`/`--set` upgrade takes the new defaults too, but drops every value it does not repeat.

With a Helm older than 3.14, hand your values back yourself, without any reuse flag:

```bash
helm get values gpustack-operator --namespace gpustack-system --output yaml > prev.yaml
helm upgrade gpustack-operator gpustack/gpustack-operator \
  --namespace gpustack-system \
  --values prev.yaml
```

## Uninstalling the Chart

To uninstall the `gpustack-operator` release:

```bash
helm uninstall gpustack-operator --namespace gpustack-system
```

`helm uninstall` removes only what the release owns. The finalizers the controllers leave on their
objects, and the CRDs, aggregated APIServices and webhook configurations the worker registers itself,
stay behind. Run the chart's `files/cleanup.sh` afterwards to clear them, or install with
`--set cleanupOnUninstall=true` to have gated hook Jobs do it — which is safe only where this release
exclusively owns those CRDs. Before anything is deleted, those Jobs drain the operator's custom
resources and, where this release owns Kueue, stop the worker and delete the Kueue objects it created
while Kueue can still finalize them; the post-delete Job then clears what is left.
On the Nodes, the cleanup deletes the NodeFeatures the operator reported and removes the
`topology.gpustack.ai/profile`, `sliced-max-free-units.fit.gpustack.ai/*` and
`shared-free-cards.fit.gpustack.ai/*` labels the worker wrote; it leaves
`gpustack.ai/managed` and the other `topology.gpustack.ai/*` labels, which an administrator may set.

The dependencies listed below are **vendored subcharts**: their sources are committed into this
chart's `charts/` directory, so the `Repository` column is intentionally empty — `helm install`
fetches nothing beyond the chart itself. Each is pinned at the version shown and sits behind its own
`<name>.enabled` switch (`kueue`, `node-feature-discovery`, `csi-driver-nfs`, `csi-driver-s3`).

